1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Sweltering heat, icy cold, and hardly a drop of water,

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,500
the death trap they call the desert.

3
00:00:11,501 --> 00:00:15,000
Yet through man's ingenuity, he's not only survived,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
but thrived here.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:19,000
The complexity of irrigation systems,

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,000
the comfort of air conditioning,

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:24,000
the promise of solar energy.

8
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
From ancient times to the present and beyond,

9
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,800
and beyond, man has transformed barren wastelands to worlds of

10
00:00:31,801 --> 00:00:32,800
wonder.

11
00:00:32,801 --> 00:00:35,800
Now, Desert Tech on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:51,200
The desert.

13
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:54,200
By day, the sun beats down hellishly without mercy.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
On a winter night, the chill wind blows through canyons

15
00:01:01,001 --> 00:01:03,000
with an icy fury.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,000
But it's really all about the water.

17
00:01:08,001 --> 00:01:12,000
Deserts typically get less than six inches of annual rainfall,

18
00:01:12,001 --> 00:01:16,000
so finding and maintaining a water supply means everything.

19
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:18,000
It's an enormous challenge.

20
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:21,000
Some 40% of the Earth's land is desert.

21
00:01:21,001 --> 00:01:25,000
So throughout time, man has, by necessity and by choice,

22
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:30,300
and by choice, had to cope with it, cross it, survive it.

23
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,240
We started out in the desert, and we've evolved

24
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:37,140
increasingly sophisticated technologies that have allowed

25
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:40,540
people to live in what are some pretty horrendous environments

26
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:44,060
by anybody's standards.

27
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:47,060
Our religions grew in desert environments.

28
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:50,400
The desert is a testing environment where you find out

29
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:51,960
what you're really made out of.

30
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:53,760
In order to live here, people have

31
00:01:53,761 --> 00:01:56,760
have had to organize, invent new technologies.

32
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:59,760
People have had to come to grips with living

33
00:01:59,860 --> 00:02:02,060
within the environment in a way that they weren't forced

34
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:03,660
to in many other parts of the world.

35
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:11,520
In the Middle East, where arid desolation

36
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:14,520
dominates the landscape, people are surrounded

37
00:02:14,620 --> 00:02:17,620
by great bodies of water.

38
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:19,760
But none of it is drinkable.

39
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:22,460
Still, desert technology allows millions

40
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:28,700
to live along the coastlines. Since the 1970s, as its oil-rich

41
00:02:28,701 --> 00:02:32,300
societies have expanded, the Middle East has increasingly

42
00:02:32,301 --> 00:02:37,900
relied on desalinization, also known as desalination.

43
00:02:37,901 --> 00:02:41,520
This is a process that removes salt from seawater.

44
00:02:41,521 --> 00:02:45,480
Seawater from five surrounding oceans and seas can be diverted

45
00:02:45,481 --> 00:02:48,780
toward the desert's populated areas for drinking, bathing,

46
00:02:48,781 --> 00:02:50,780
and irrigation.

47
00:02:50,781 --> 00:02:55,980
About 90% to 95% of the water in the world is salty water,

48
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:58,160
and you can't use it.

49
00:02:58,260 --> 00:03:02,360
Desalination has to be a part of our long-term water resource

50
00:03:02,460 --> 00:03:05,520
management strategy.

51
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:08,500
The salt content of seawater is approximately 33,000

52
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:11,840
to 34,000 parts per million.

53
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,400
That content must be reduced to just 350 parts per million

54
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,680
to make it drinkable.

55
00:03:18,681 --> 00:03:26,180
To accomplish this, desalination plants run a widely used system called reverse osmosis.

56
00:03:26,181 --> 00:03:31,740
After water is pretreated to remove common marine debris, it flows through a pressurizing

57
00:03:31,741 --> 00:03:37,060
unit and then travels through a filter porous enough for water to flow through, but too

58
00:03:37,061 --> 00:03:44,040
small for salt molecules to penetrate. Once completed, the process yields a waste

59
00:03:44,041 --> 00:03:48,040
of salty water that must be discarded.

60
00:03:48,041 --> 00:03:51,040
So the question is, what do you do with the salty water?

61
00:03:51,041 --> 00:03:54,040
Living near the ocean or the sea, you can dispose of this

62
00:03:54,041 --> 00:03:56,040
right back into the ocean and the sea.

63
00:03:56,041 --> 00:03:59,040
So that's why that makes sense in the Middle East.

64
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:04,040
Desalination requires a lot of energy

65
00:04:04,041 --> 00:04:07,040
and is therefore extremely expensive.

66
00:04:07,041 --> 00:04:12,040
It costs over $650 per acre-foot to desalinate seawater,

67
00:04:12,041 --> 00:04:17,840
water, as compared to about $100 per acre foot to purify water from conventional freshwater

68
00:04:17,841 --> 00:04:24,040
reservoirs. But when you have no choice, as in

69
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:29,240
the desert, the cost is unavoidable. So only really in situations where

70
00:04:29,241 --> 00:04:35,040
energy is cheap and the demand to live in a place without surface water is large, doesn't

71
00:04:35,041 --> 00:04:41,340
make sense to do it. In ancient times, without the benefit

72
00:04:41,341 --> 00:04:45,140
of desalination technology. The only parts of the desert where

73
00:04:45,141 --> 00:04:48,980
people could live permanently were those that bordered rivers or were

74
00:04:48,981 --> 00:04:55,420
situated on oases. An oasis is a desert area where water

75
00:04:55,421 --> 00:05:01,980
from an aquifer, an underground pool, has seeped to the surface.

76
00:05:01,981 --> 00:05:05,660
Societies could also settle in areas separated from the water by

77
00:05:05,661 --> 00:05:12,660
rivers to their remote locations. 6,000 years ago, in the harsh

78
00:05:12,661 --> 00:05:17,660
environment of ancient Mesopotamia, a resourceful culture thrived for

79
00:05:17,661 --> 00:05:19,660
millennia. They conquered the desert by

80
00:05:19,661 --> 00:05:23,660
ingeniously utilizing the waters of the Euphrates and Tigris rivers.

81
00:05:23,661 --> 00:05:28,660
The region today is the site of modern-day Iraq.

82
00:05:28,661 --> 00:05:32,660
They were able, primarily through irrigation, to produce tremendous

83
00:05:32,661 --> 00:05:35,600
produce tremendous quantities of agricultural food,

84
00:05:35,601 --> 00:05:39,060
and they were really converting what otherwise, to you and I,

85
00:05:39,061 --> 00:05:40,960
would seem like a tremendous desert,

86
00:05:40,961 --> 00:05:44,600
into agricultural fields as far as the eye could see.

87
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,620
But perhaps their most spectacular achievement

88
00:05:51,621 --> 00:05:54,720
was the creation of the Hanging Gardens of Babylon,

89
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:56,760
one of the seven wonders of the ancient world.

90
00:05:56,761 --> 00:06:01,760
Since Babylon, the capital of Babylonia,

91
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:04,660
was a desert city with little rainfall,

92
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,500
the gardens had to be irrigated.

93
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:12,000
The source of the garden's water was the Euphrates River.

94
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:15,920
The water was dispersed by a slave-powered chain pump,

95
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:19,120
which had two large wheels like a ski lift.

96
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,420
Ceramic pots or buckets hanging from the chain

97
00:06:22,520 --> 00:06:24,520
were continuously dipped into the reservoir

98
00:06:24,521 --> 00:06:27,160
reservoir at the base of the gardens.

99
00:06:27,260 --> 00:06:29,760
Once the water reached the top of the gardens,

100
00:06:29,860 --> 00:06:34,060
it was released from gates into irrigation channels.

101
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:38,960
Those gardens also created evaporative cooling,

102
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:41,280
so that, in fact, it was a wonderfully cool place

103
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:44,880
to be, even in a hot, dry desert.

104
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:47,780
The renowned gardens were just a part of ancient Babylonia's

105
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:51,180
architectural strategy to combat the scorching desert

106
00:06:51,181 --> 00:06:54,380
a touching desert climate, where temperatures could rise

107
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,380
as high as 120 degrees.

108
00:07:03,220 --> 00:07:05,620
They found ways of creating a lot of shade.

109
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:08,740
They found ways of grabbing whatever wind there was

110
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:11,440
and channeling it down into the houses.

111
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:15,180
They built with very thick walls and very thick roofs.

112
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:18,080
Many, many what we would call passive technologies

113
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:19,680
were used in those days.

114
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:24,620
Specially designed Babylonian homes help the people stay cool

115
00:07:24,621 --> 00:07:29,620
in the desert. They have stood the test of time.

116
00:07:29,621 --> 00:07:33,940
Courtyard houses featured shady plants and trees, and typically,

117
00:07:33,941 --> 00:07:38,120
a pool. As the water evaporated, it created cool air in the

118
00:07:38,121 --> 00:07:41,220
courtyard, which could then flow inside the home through several

119
00:07:41,221 --> 00:07:43,420
open doorways. One of the wonderful things about

120
00:07:43,421 --> 00:07:47,040
the desert is you get this big change in the temperature from

121
00:07:47,041 --> 00:07:50,520
day to night, as much as 30 degrees Fahrenheit, so that they

122
00:07:50,521 --> 00:07:54,600
can throw those doors open and get that cool night air into

123
00:07:54,601 --> 00:07:57,000
those rooms.

124
00:07:57,001 --> 00:07:59,900
Babylonian architects helped home dwellers beat the heat

125
00:07:59,901 --> 00:08:02,640
with another design, reminiscent of the biblical

126
00:08:02,641 --> 00:08:04,760
Tower of Babel.

127
00:08:04,761 --> 00:08:08,600
The tower house, still popular today, was typically eight or

128
00:08:08,601 --> 00:08:11,800
nine stories, and people lived on the top.

129
00:08:11,801 --> 00:08:14,180
First of all, you have access to the cross breezes when you

130
00:08:14,181 --> 00:08:15,420
get up high.

131
00:08:15,421 --> 00:08:20,180
Secondly, because of the cold night air, it cools off very

132
00:08:20,181 --> 00:08:21,380
at night.

133
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:24,860
Then on top of that, the very tops of these houses

134
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:27,120
are covered with a white plaster.

135
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:29,020
And that's highly reflective.

136
00:08:29,120 --> 00:08:33,220
So in fact, they don't gather much heat at the top at all.

137
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:36,160
As the Mesopotamians flourished in the hostile desert,

138
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:39,160
they found that over time, their ingenious use of water

139
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:42,160
carried a high environmental price.

140
00:08:42,260 --> 00:08:45,480
They probably suffered very much from what some parts

141
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:47,100
of Southern California suffer from,

142
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:49,680
and that is salinization, where too much salt

143
00:08:49,681 --> 00:08:54,480
the soil. The basic story is that if you bring too much water to

144
00:08:54,481 --> 00:08:57,920
the land, a lot of it evaporates, leaving the minerals in the

145
00:08:57,921 --> 00:09:01,920
ground. Eventually, you can't grow crops on it.

146
00:09:01,921 --> 00:09:05,420
Ironically, the Mesopotamian skill at creating and utilizing

147
00:09:05,421 --> 00:09:09,040
desert technology proved their undoing.

148
00:09:09,041 --> 00:09:13,040
They pushed the environment beyond what it could sustain.

149
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:18,880
This is the kind of decision that I think the past

150
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:21,780
illuminates for us. The greatest, most gifted people

151
00:09:21,880 --> 00:09:26,320
also fell prey to short-term maximization without respect

152
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:28,420
to the consequences for the long term.

153
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:33,820
Thousands of years after the Babylonians' efforts

154
00:09:33,920 --> 00:09:36,940
to beat back the desert, modern developers

155
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:40,540
would create desert cities like Phoenix, Arizona,

156
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:42,640
that would also push the arid environments

157
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:44,940
to the limits of sustainability.

158
00:09:48,880 --> 00:10:00,540
In the last 50 years or so, Americans have found ways not to

159
00:10:00,541 --> 00:10:03,580
just survive in the desert, but thrive.

160
00:10:03,581 --> 00:10:06,720
And yet, there is a double-edged sword.

161
00:10:06,721 --> 00:10:10,420
The story of Phoenix, Arizona is a cautionary tale of how people

162
00:10:10,421 --> 00:10:12,780
can create and implement technology to conquer the

163
00:10:12,781 --> 00:10:16,280
desert, but in the process, threaten the environment that

164
00:10:16,281 --> 00:10:17,280
sustains them.

165
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:28,760
We use hundreds of gallons a day per person in Phoenix.

166
00:10:28,761 --> 00:10:31,820
And that kind of irresponsibility is something

167
00:10:31,821 --> 00:10:33,600
we really need to think seriously about.

168
00:10:37,540 --> 00:10:41,140
From 1980 to 2000, the Phoenix metro area

169
00:10:41,141 --> 00:10:44,340
doubled in size.

170
00:10:44,341 --> 00:10:48,440
It now bursts with economic opportunity, sprawling with homes,

171
00:10:48,441 --> 00:10:54,440
hotels, and highways. With a population of over 3 million,

172
00:10:54,441 --> 00:10:58,440
it's one of the fastest-growing regions in America, with no sign of

173
00:10:58,441 --> 00:11:06,440
slowing down. Phoenix, like ancient Mesopotamia,

174
00:11:06,441 --> 00:11:12,440
has scant rainfall, averaging less than 8 inches a year.

175
00:11:12,441 --> 00:11:15,200
But like the ancient desert civilization which flourished in

176
00:11:15,201 --> 00:11:18,880
proximity to the Tigris and the Euphrates, Phoenix gets water

177
00:11:18,881 --> 00:11:24,280
from a river, in this case, the Salt River.

178
00:11:24,281 --> 00:11:27,440
It's so named because of its alkaline content, which

179
00:11:27,441 --> 00:11:29,840
nonetheless doesn't prohibit human consumption.

180
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:36,180
A thousand years before the first white settlers ever stepped

181
00:11:36,181 --> 00:11:41,180
foot onto the hot, sandy soil, Native Americans thrived here,

182
00:11:41,181 --> 00:11:44,880
strong, they found a way to reclaim the desert and prosper

183
00:11:44,881 --> 00:11:48,080
in the process. The Hohokam Indians had over

184
00:11:48,081 --> 00:11:52,420
1,000 linear miles of irrigation canals.

185
00:11:52,421 --> 00:11:55,520
Archaeologists today are still amazed at the sophisticated

186
00:11:55,521 --> 00:12:00,140
nature of their engineering that went into their canal systems.

187
00:12:00,141 --> 00:12:04,140
The Hohokam were able to take water away from the several

188
00:12:04,141 --> 00:12:08,940
perennial rivers that existed in the desert and transport it

189
00:12:08,941 --> 00:12:14,080
and transport it dozens of miles to large villages that were

190
00:12:14,081 --> 00:12:19,680
tending to thousands of acres of farmland.

191
00:12:19,681 --> 00:12:23,680
However, like the Mesopotamians, the Hohokam culture eventually

192
00:12:23,681 --> 00:12:27,600
died out. Did the desert defeat them?

193
00:12:27,601 --> 00:12:30,660
With scant evidence, historians can only speculate.

194
00:12:30,661 --> 00:12:33,960
They face the same challenges that we face today.

195
00:12:33,961 --> 00:12:38,060
How do you get along in a marginal desert environment?

196
00:12:38,061 --> 00:12:41,360
How do you use the most precious resource of that environment?

197
00:12:41,640 --> 00:12:42,340
It's water.

198
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:46,020
These issues that are important today were important to them,

199
00:12:46,060 --> 00:12:49,280
and the better we understand on how they tackle those challenges,

200
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:51,940
I think the better prepared we'll be for our future.

201
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:57,020
The first white settlers who tried to tame Arizona's desert

202
00:12:57,021 --> 00:13:01,220
in the mid-19th century picked up where the Hohokam left off.

203
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:05,000
And they got the brilliant idea that they could re-dig that canal system

204
00:13:05,001 --> 00:13:09,960
and they could make the desert flourish again with various agricultural products.

205
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:17,080
And behind me to the left is a historic canal that was excavated in 1878.

206
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:22,740
And this canal was one of the very first ones that delivered water to thousands of acres of farmland

207
00:13:22,741 --> 00:13:24,380
to the earlier American settlers.

208
00:13:24,780 --> 00:13:27,620
And it's still in use today, well over 100 years later.

209
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:34,400
When it came time to name their town, the settlers reasoned they were creating something new

210
00:13:34,401 --> 00:13:38,200
ashes of the past. Inspired by the mythological bird

211
00:13:38,201 --> 00:13:41,740
which did exactly that, they deemed Phoenix an appropriate

212
00:13:41,741 --> 00:13:44,740
choice. For the next century and a half,

213
00:13:44,741 --> 00:13:48,740
the city would live up to its name. It was a boomtown, but a

214
00:13:48,741 --> 00:13:51,760
boomtown based on land and farming and irrigation water.

215
00:13:51,761 --> 00:13:55,760
They organized themselves. They lobbied in Washington.

216
00:13:55,761 --> 00:13:58,500
The settlers needed the government's help, and the

217
00:13:58,501 --> 00:14:00,760
government wanted to settle the West.

218
00:14:00,761 --> 00:14:04,400
As a result, the U.S. Bureau of Reclamation was established.

219
00:14:04,401 --> 00:14:07,440
with the specific goal of providing water to Southwestern

220
00:14:07,441 --> 00:14:10,440
farmers. The first major U.S.

221
00:14:10,441 --> 00:14:14,040
reclamation project was the construction of Roosevelt Dam to

222
00:14:14,041 --> 00:14:17,840
impound the waters of the Salt River, to make it available for

223
00:14:17,841 --> 00:14:21,560
irrigation in a more orderly fashion, and just multiplying

224
00:14:21,561 --> 00:14:25,160
the potential. A dam of this size had never

225
00:14:25,161 --> 00:14:30,160
been constructed. It was 280 feet high and 723

226
00:14:30,161 --> 00:14:33,660
long. Furthermore, the project marked

227
00:14:33,661 --> 00:14:37,660
the beginning of hydroelectric energy production.

228
00:14:37,661 --> 00:14:41,400
Now with water and power, and lots of inexpensive land, Phoenix

229
00:14:41,401 --> 00:14:45,600
had nearly all the basic building blocks for a modern community.

230
00:14:45,601 --> 00:14:50,620
Except that it was a desert with unbearably hot summers.

231
00:14:50,621 --> 00:14:54,160
So when air conditioning was introduced decades later, after

232
00:14:54,161 --> 00:14:58,160
World War II, it made all the difference in the world.

233
00:14:58,161 --> 00:15:01,600
Gettle air conditioning was the first to adapt Willis Carrier's

234
00:15:01,601 --> 00:15:05,200
1902 invention to the Phoenix area.

235
00:15:05,201 --> 00:15:08,900
Its units weren't cheap, but they did the job.

236
00:15:08,901 --> 00:15:11,100
An air conditioner is basically a refrigerator

237
00:15:11,101 --> 00:15:13,820
without the insulated box.

238
00:15:13,821 --> 00:15:18,460
A compressor pressurizes a refrigerant, usually free on gas.

239
00:15:18,461 --> 00:15:20,760
The pressure forces it to heat up.

240
00:15:20,761 --> 00:15:23,220
This hot gas runs through a set of coils

241
00:15:23,221 --> 00:15:28,120
in order to dissipate its heat, condensing it into a liquid.

242
00:15:28,121 --> 00:15:31,480
The liquefied freon then runs through an expansion valve,

243
00:15:31,481 --> 00:15:35,980
where it evaporates to become cold, low-pressure freon gas.

244
00:15:35,981 --> 00:15:38,260
This cold gas runs through a set of coils

245
00:15:38,261 --> 00:15:41,320
that allows the gas to absorb heat and cool down the air

246
00:15:41,321 --> 00:15:42,220
inside the building.

247
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:48,580
In the 1960s, air conditioning technology like this

248
00:15:48,581 --> 00:15:51,820
became mass-produced and affordable.

249
00:15:51,821 --> 00:15:54,420
That changed everything for Phoenix.

250
00:15:54,421 --> 00:15:56,760
People no longer perceived its desert climate

251
00:15:56,761 --> 00:15:57,980
as a drawback.

252
00:15:57,981 --> 00:16:03,420
We have three months a year here where the average high temperature is well above 100 degrees.

253
00:16:04,520 --> 00:16:11,080
Air conditioning, today most of us wake up in an air-conditioned home, get into an air-conditioned car,

254
00:16:11,160 --> 00:16:16,460
go to our air-conditioned offices, and it may be 110 or even 120 degrees outside,

255
00:16:16,700 --> 00:16:20,620
but we live in a world that's forever 75 degrees Fahrenheit.

256
00:16:21,520 --> 00:16:26,880
You could really weather this heat, and then the rest of the year it was spectacular out,

257
00:16:26,881 --> 00:16:29,280
And so you were happy to be here.

258
00:16:29,380 --> 00:16:31,780
But was it too much of a good thing?

259
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:35,580
With a population that rose from over 300,000 in 1950

260
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:39,520
to over 3 million by 2000, the climate of Phoenix

261
00:16:39,620 --> 00:16:41,520
actually changed.

262
00:16:41,620 --> 00:16:44,840
Population growth has really created two changes

263
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:45,940
in our climate.

264
00:16:46,040 --> 00:16:49,140
First is humidity, because of the swimming pools

265
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:51,980
and the irrigated golf courses and front lawns and things

266
00:16:52,080 --> 00:16:53,140
like that.

267
00:16:53,141 --> 00:16:57,940
several points higher average humidity here in Phoenix

268
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:00,940
than originally existed.

269
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:03,580
Phoenix's second climate change is a phenomenon

270
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:06,380
called the Heat Island effect.

271
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:10,800
All the heat from the solar radiation during the day

272
00:17:10,900 --> 00:17:14,700
is stored in our parking lots and in our roads,

273
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:17,100
and it just emanates overnight.

274
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:19,100
And one of the big problems that has

275
00:17:19,101 --> 00:17:23,100
is that the nighttime temperature of many of our desert cities

276
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:26,440
has gone up as much as 15 degrees Fahrenheit

277
00:17:26,540 --> 00:17:28,940
from what it used to be.

278
00:17:29,040 --> 00:17:31,740
As the 21st century dawned, Phoenix

279
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:33,740
had become trapped in a vicious cycle.

280
00:17:33,840 --> 00:17:37,660
That 15 degrees has got to be swallowed by air conditioning.

281
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:38,960
And that air conditioning, of course,

282
00:17:39,060 --> 00:17:42,360
lets even more heat out into that night air.

283
00:17:42,460 --> 00:17:44,360
So we've got a very serious problem there.

284
00:17:44,460 --> 00:17:47,000
While these problems didn't develop overnight,

285
00:17:47,001 --> 00:17:51,480
anticipated as early as 1970. There was concern in some

286
00:17:51,481 --> 00:17:54,600
circles that if the result of all this desert tech was a

287
00:17:54,601 --> 00:17:58,280
generally flat urban sprawl, then perhaps some rethinking was

288
00:17:58,281 --> 00:18:01,320
in order. This led to the creation of an

289
00:18:01,321 --> 00:18:04,360
experimental alternative community called Arcosanti,

290
00:18:04,361 --> 00:18:08,760
located 70 miles north of Phoenix. A collection of

291
00:18:08,761 --> 00:18:12,360
eclectic, densely packed buildings, it was steeped in

292
00:18:12,361 --> 00:18:15,800
the kind of desert tech employed by the ancient Mesopotamians,

293
00:18:15,801 --> 00:18:18,800
low-tech but effective. The sun is rising out here to

294
00:18:18,801 --> 00:18:21,800
the northeast, arcs high in the sky and sets over here to the

295
00:18:21,801 --> 00:18:24,800
northwest, so the entire building shades right out to this curve,

296
00:18:24,801 --> 00:18:27,800
in fact. It is passive solar architecture.

297
00:18:27,801 --> 00:18:30,800
The building is really working with the annual regimen of the

298
00:18:30,801 --> 00:18:33,800
sun to create an environment that is a warm environment for the

299
00:18:33,801 --> 00:18:38,800
winter and then a shaded environment for the summer.

300
00:18:38,801 --> 00:18:42,800
Parcosanti was the brainchild of Italian-born architect Paolo

301
00:18:42,801 --> 00:18:46,400
Soleri, who felt Phoenix was conquering the desert instead of

302
00:18:46,401 --> 00:18:49,600
adapting to it. Phoenix is indifferent to the

303
00:18:49,601 --> 00:18:52,480
desert, so I had my alternative, which I call it the lean

304
00:18:52,481 --> 00:18:55,760
alternative. Among other things,

305
00:18:55,761 --> 00:18:58,960
Soleri felt the need for air conditioning could be minimized by

306
00:18:58,961 --> 00:19:01,520
construction materials that could offset extreme desert

307
00:19:01,521 --> 00:19:06,160
temperatures. In the desert, the primary

308
00:19:06,161 --> 00:19:09,840
surface that you want to be aware of for insulation is going to be

309
00:19:09,841 --> 00:19:12,320
the roof, the horizontal surface, especially in the heat of the

310
00:19:12,321 --> 00:19:15,220
summer. This has about four inches of

311
00:19:15,221 --> 00:19:19,020
sprayed foam insulation, which is very effective at mitigating

312
00:19:19,021 --> 00:19:22,660
overheating in a concrete structure. Besides adapting to

313
00:19:22,661 --> 00:19:26,960
the sun, Arcosanti's oddly shaped roofs also maximize water

314
00:19:26,961 --> 00:19:30,540
conservation. Water captured during sudden

315
00:19:30,541 --> 00:19:33,440
downpours drains down to collection vessels.

316
00:19:33,441 --> 00:19:37,420
What we're trying to do here is to use what water we have

317
00:19:37,421 --> 00:19:41,520
responsibly. Despite its common sense

318
00:19:41,521 --> 00:19:46,120
to desert tech, Arcosante failed to catch on, never growing beyond

319
00:19:46,121 --> 00:19:48,920
a community of several dozen people.

320
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:52,960
Meanwhile, Phoenix continued to explode, but its very success

321
00:19:52,961 --> 00:19:57,900
led to serious water issues. Problems developed in Phoenix's

322
00:19:57,901 --> 00:20:01,580
aquifer, where underground water was trapped millions of years

323
00:20:01,581 --> 00:20:04,580
ago. Phoenix obtained a

324
00:20:04,581 --> 00:20:08,080
considerable amount of their water resources from groundwater.

325
00:20:08,081 --> 00:20:11,920
Historically, over time, this has resulted in rather

326
00:20:12,020 --> 00:20:16,380
significant drawdowns in the aquifer, ranging anywhere

327
00:20:16,480 --> 00:20:18,420
between 50 and 100 feet.

328
00:20:18,520 --> 00:20:21,120
This has caused subsidence in some parts of the valley.

329
00:20:21,220 --> 00:20:23,820
For example, over at Luke Air Force Base,

330
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:26,840
it's dropped nearly 18 feet.

331
00:20:26,940 --> 00:20:29,740
In other words, Phoenix is sinking

332
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:33,240
and has been for decades.

333
00:20:33,340 --> 00:20:36,680
Arizona pretty much was persuaded or forced

334
00:20:36,681 --> 00:20:39,923
are forced by the federal government to come to grips with this.

335
00:20:39,924 --> 00:20:41,240
And it instigated a really

336
00:20:41,241 --> 00:20:46,400
innovative law called the Groundwater Management Act that in fact put all sorts of constraints

337
00:20:46,401 --> 00:20:51,546
on new farming. It put incentives for water conservation among the cities.

338
00:20:51,547 --> 00:20:52,380
And it set a

339
00:20:52,381 --> 00:20:56,460
goal of balancing natural recharge with extraction rate.

340
00:20:57,680 --> 00:21:03,380
Man should always strive to be in balance with the desert because it is a challenging

341
00:21:03,381 --> 00:21:06,780
to live in. We are increasing our population size,

342
00:21:06,880 --> 00:21:11,180
and therefore the carrying capacity of the land.

343
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:13,280
And so we need to be smart about it.

344
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:25,640
In the 21st century, surviving a journey or camping

345
00:21:25,740 --> 00:21:28,880
in a harsh environment of the desert isn't just possible.

346
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:29,980
It's the norm.

347
00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:36,220
A variety of survival gear is readily available at one's

348
00:21:36,320 --> 00:21:37,820
local outdoor outfitters.

349
00:21:40,920 --> 00:21:43,620
These modern methods have their roots in the time-tested ways

350
00:21:43,720 --> 00:21:47,520
of the Middle East's nomadic Bedouin tribes.

351
00:21:47,620 --> 00:21:52,040
The traditional Bedouins were the ultimate in desert dwellers.

352
00:21:52,140 --> 00:21:56,340
They were able to adapt by being mobile,

353
00:21:56,440 --> 00:22:00,480
by practicing seasonal movements of their flocks,

354
00:22:00,481 --> 00:22:04,480
with a relatively low technology.

355
00:22:04,580 --> 00:22:07,320
Bedouins could travel on their camels hundreds of miles

356
00:22:07,420 --> 00:22:09,980
across the desert. Part of their secret

357
00:22:10,080 --> 00:22:12,920
was knowing that the seemingly barren landscape wasn't quite

358
00:22:13,020 --> 00:22:16,460
as barren as it appeared. Knowledge of the locations

359
00:22:16,560 --> 00:22:18,540
of water sources across the desert

360
00:22:18,640 --> 00:22:21,140
were passed down from father to son.

361
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:24,480
Even today, you ask some Bedouin people living in southern Jordan,

362
00:22:24,580 --> 00:22:26,580
for example, they know where to find water.

363
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:29,980
They can dig holes in the bottom of wadis or streams

364
00:22:29,981 --> 00:22:35,920
and usually find some amount of water to subsist on.

365
00:22:36,020 --> 00:22:39,020
Depending on the area, water could be found by digging

366
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:41,220
relatively shallow holes.

367
00:22:41,320 --> 00:22:43,220
And because water found in the desert sand

368
00:22:43,320 --> 00:22:46,100
is typically too hot for bacteria to live in,

369
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:49,040
it usually doesn't require purification,

370
00:22:49,140 --> 00:22:52,600
only straining through cloth to remove sediment.

371
00:22:52,700 --> 00:22:56,140
With life-giving water, the Bedouins could survive.

372
00:22:56,240 --> 00:22:59,980
But what about exposure to the sun?

373
00:22:59,981 --> 00:23:01,820
The Bedouins' clothing, consisting

374
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:05,180
of a head cloth called a keffiyeh and a dark tunic,

375
00:23:05,280 --> 00:23:07,580
covers them from head to toe.

376
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:09,920
This protects them from the sun.

377
00:23:10,020 --> 00:23:13,320
The loose-fitting tunic also keeps them cool by allowing air

378
00:23:13,420 --> 00:23:14,820
to circulate.

379
00:23:14,920 --> 00:23:17,540
But because the air is trapped within the material,

380
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:20,940
it also prevents their sweat from evaporating too quickly,

381
00:23:21,040 --> 00:23:23,940
thus slowing down dehydration in the hot, dry air.

382
00:23:23,941 --> 00:23:27,740
The Bedouin's simple tents, like their clothing,

383
00:23:27,780 --> 00:23:32,420
belied an ingenious low-tech design tailored for desert survival.

384
00:23:32,460 --> 00:23:34,580
They are made out of camel hair.

385
00:23:34,620 --> 00:23:38,020
And those tents are fairly loosely woven

386
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:42,420
so that there's plenty of air that gets through the tent.

387
00:23:42,460 --> 00:23:44,420
It isn't an impervious layer.

388
00:23:44,460 --> 00:23:47,940
It is actually allowing the air to flow through it

389
00:23:47,980 --> 00:23:49,940
in a very free way.

390
00:23:53,940 --> 00:23:59,140
At the heart of every Bedouin's desert survival strategy is the

391
00:23:59,141 --> 00:24:01,540
camel. The camel is this

392
00:24:01,541 --> 00:24:05,140
extraordinary animal. Not only is it able to survive

393
00:24:05,141 --> 00:24:09,140
without regular water, but it carries very considerable loads.

394
00:24:09,141 --> 00:24:15,140
Camels can carry their Bedouin riders across the Middle

395
00:24:15,141 --> 00:24:21,140
Eastern desert without water for 25 days in the winter, five in

396
00:24:21,141 --> 00:24:23,140
in the summer heat.

397
00:24:30,780 --> 00:24:34,220
In America, most people travel across the desert in air

398
00:24:34,320 --> 00:24:36,320
conditioned comfort on super highways.

399
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:44,160
But the desert has also become a popular place to hike, camp,

400
00:24:47,100 --> 00:24:50,440
or ride in off-road vehicles.

401
00:24:50,441 --> 00:24:54,520
How does one survive a day or two in the desert?

402
00:24:54,521 --> 00:24:57,880
Proper preparation is vital.

403
00:24:57,881 --> 00:25:01,560
The most basic supply is water, carried either in a canteen

404
00:25:01,561 --> 00:25:04,020
or the more recent hydration pack.

405
00:25:04,021 --> 00:25:05,880
But what if you run out?

406
00:25:05,881 --> 00:25:08,460
You can find water by looking to nature.

407
00:25:08,461 --> 00:25:12,220
Flies and mosquitoes are a sure sign water is in the vicinity.

408
00:25:12,221 --> 00:25:15,660
And bees will fly to and from water in a straight line

409
00:25:15,661 --> 00:25:17,560
up to 1,000 yards away.

410
00:25:17,561 --> 00:25:19,160
And what you're going to need to do

411
00:25:19,161 --> 00:25:21,400
is actually capture that water,

412
00:25:21,401 --> 00:25:23,860
but also you're going to need to clean that water

413
00:25:23,861 --> 00:25:25,860
through a water purifier.

414
00:25:25,861 --> 00:25:28,760
And the way that it's used is you actually just unscrew the bottom,

415
00:25:28,761 --> 00:25:32,900
and it screws onto the top of the bottle.

416
00:25:32,901 --> 00:25:36,420
And then once that's in the water, you just begin to pump.

417
00:25:36,421 --> 00:25:38,520
At this point, the water's going to come up through the tube

418
00:25:38,521 --> 00:25:40,020
and down into the bottle,

419
00:25:40,021 --> 00:25:44,560
and you can drink it after it comes out through the filter.

420
00:25:44,561 --> 00:25:47,120
Besides water, another key to desert survival

421
00:25:47,121 --> 00:25:50,620
is protecting your skin. The desert has an abundance of

422
00:25:50,621 --> 00:25:54,620
sunlight that carries harmful ultraviolet radiation.

423
00:25:54,621 --> 00:25:57,460
The ultraviolet light that reaches the Earth's surface is

424
00:25:57,461 --> 00:26:00,840
really three types. There's UVA, there's UVB, and

425
00:26:00,841 --> 00:26:03,740
there's UVC. The majority of what reaches the

426
00:26:03,741 --> 00:26:07,500
Earth's surface is the long wavelength UVA radiation, and

427
00:26:07,501 --> 00:26:10,980
that does have damaging effects on the skin for aging the skin

428
00:26:10,981 --> 00:26:15,220
and causing skin cancers. The UVB is the middle range of

429
00:26:15,221 --> 00:26:19,960
of wavelengths, which tends to cause sunburns and skin cancers.

430
00:26:19,961 --> 00:26:23,920
UVC really doesn't play a significant role in your day-to-day

431
00:26:23,921 --> 00:26:28,220
exposure. The first line of defense is

432
00:26:28,221 --> 00:26:31,860
protective clothing. The big myth is that regular

433
00:26:31,861 --> 00:26:34,840
clothing will protect you from sun exposure.

434
00:26:34,841 --> 00:26:38,280
In fact, a regular men's white cotton shirt or cotton t-shirt

435
00:26:38,281 --> 00:26:41,260
only has an SPF rating of around 6.

436
00:26:41,261 --> 00:26:44,580
More darkly colored clothing or more tightly woven clothing will

437
00:26:44,581 --> 00:26:47,340
protect you to a stronger degree.

438
00:26:47,341 --> 00:26:50,660
But just your regular daily street clothing,

439
00:26:50,661 --> 00:26:53,280
you're still getting UV exposure through that.

440
00:26:53,281 --> 00:26:54,660
When you're out in the desert,

441
00:26:54,661 --> 00:26:56,860
you're going to be exposed to the sun and very high heat.

442
00:26:56,861 --> 00:26:59,000
And one of the best ways of protecting yourself

443
00:26:59,001 --> 00:27:01,200
and keeping yourself safe is by the clothing

444
00:27:01,201 --> 00:27:02,740
that you actually wear.

445
00:27:02,741 --> 00:27:05,940
This hat right here is actually referred to as the Sahara hat.

446
00:27:05,941 --> 00:27:08,900
And what makes this hat special is that it does block the sun,

447
00:27:08,901 --> 00:27:12,240
but it does have an ultraviolet protection of 50 on it.

448
00:27:12,241 --> 00:27:15,780
It has a very wide brim, a four inch on the front,

449
00:27:15,880 --> 00:27:20,180
and it has a veil on the back to protect my neck.

450
00:27:20,280 --> 00:27:22,580
In addition to hats, outdoor outfitters

451
00:27:22,680 --> 00:27:25,480
sell shirts and pants that have been chemically treated

452
00:27:25,580 --> 00:27:29,300
and have an SPF factor of 50.

453
00:27:29,400 --> 00:27:33,300
Sunglasses that block out UV rays will save your eyes,

454
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:37,000
and a good sunscreen will literally save your skin.

455
00:27:37,100 --> 00:27:39,500
Two varieties of sunscreens help protect skin

456
00:27:39,600 --> 00:27:41,540
in two different ways.

457
00:27:41,541 --> 00:27:44,540
chemical sunscreen acts by converting the ultraviolet rays

458
00:27:44,541 --> 00:27:48,940
into heat, which simply dissipates into the air.

459
00:27:48,941 --> 00:27:51,780
The physical blocking agents just sit on the skin.

460
00:27:51,781 --> 00:27:54,480
They don't interact with the light for the most part.

461
00:27:54,481 --> 00:27:57,480
They just reflect it back off the skin surface.

462
00:27:57,481 --> 00:27:59,060
The better sunscreens on the market

463
00:27:59,061 --> 00:28:01,700
have a combination of both the physical blocking agents

464
00:28:01,701 --> 00:28:03,360
and the chemical sunscreens.

465
00:28:03,361 --> 00:28:05,540
That way you can get the broadest spectrum of coverage

466
00:28:05,541 --> 00:28:10,300
for both UVA and UVB exposure.

467
00:28:10,301 --> 00:28:12,840
Ultimately, the best way to survive in the desert

468
00:28:12,940 --> 00:28:16,140
is to prepare for extreme sun, extreme heat,

469
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:17,440
and extreme dehydration.

470
00:28:23,140 --> 00:28:26,960
But if your vehicle breaks down or you get lost,

471
00:28:27,060 --> 00:28:30,160
your only hope may be if someone alerts the Search

472
00:28:30,260 --> 00:28:32,060
and Rescue Team.

473
00:28:40,300 --> 00:28:44,500
Outside the town of Barstow, California, deep in the Mojave

474
00:28:44,501 --> 00:28:48,300
Desert, weekend warriors like to immerse themselves in Desert

475
00:28:48,301 --> 00:28:51,140
Tech. They get out on their ATVs or

476
00:28:51,141 --> 00:28:54,240
dune buggies for some excitement and fun.

477
00:28:54,241 --> 00:29:00,360
But sometimes a miscalculation can put lives in jeopardy.

478
00:29:00,361 --> 00:29:03,260
The desert is a harsh environment, which outdoor

479
00:29:03,261 --> 00:29:06,300
enthusiasts sometimes fail to respect.

480
00:29:06,301 --> 00:29:09,860
Unfortunately, a lot of times these individuals become

481
00:29:09,861 --> 00:29:13,260
by the elements, and it results in death.

482
00:29:13,261 --> 00:29:16,760
A lot of times our missions are recovery missions more than a

483
00:29:16,761 --> 00:29:21,300
rescue mission. Barstow's Sheriff's

484
00:29:21,301 --> 00:29:24,300
Department, the Reserve Off-Highway Vehicle Enforcement

485
00:29:24,301 --> 00:29:28,220
Unit, and the Search and Rescue Team stand ready to launch any

486
00:29:28,221 --> 00:29:32,620
necessary life-saving operation. Every report of a lost or

487
00:29:32,621 --> 00:29:36,220
missing dune buggy rider. They stay sharp by staging

488
00:29:36,221 --> 00:29:39,820
mock rescue missions. A typical scenario would be

489
00:29:39,821 --> 00:29:43,220
a group of campers possibly out for a weekend event.

490
00:29:43,221 --> 00:29:47,260
One of the campers may go for a ride on an ATV or possibly even

491
00:29:47,261 --> 00:29:51,860
a hike. And they can't quite simply can't find their way back

492
00:29:51,861 --> 00:29:55,860
to their camp. And they continue to travel many times in the

493
00:29:55,861 --> 00:29:59,580
wrong direction, which just takes them farther away from any help.

494
00:30:03,580 --> 00:30:06,520
This mock rescue operation, like most real emergency

495
00:30:06,521 --> 00:30:09,560
situations starts with a missing person call to the

496
00:30:09,561 --> 00:30:13,020
sheriff's station. They just

497
00:30:13,021 --> 00:30:15,960
underestimate the environment. They come from more populated

498
00:30:15,961 --> 00:30:19,560
areas where one block to the next is easy to locate. They get

499
00:30:19,561 --> 00:30:22,760
in this environment of thousands and thousands of square miles of

500
00:30:22,761 --> 00:30:26,840
very rural land, and they just underestimate the capabilities of

